# Appendix — Engineering Notes, Sovereignty Path, and the Honest Bottom Line

A few last items that don't fit cleanly into the four parts but
matter for SIGIL's trajectory.

## On the Cranelift bootstrap dependency

The user explicitly accepts Cranelift as a bootstrap. The honest
engineering observation: Cranelift's e-graph mid-end (Fallin 2023's
ægraphs) is the *exact same mechanism* that should live at SIGIL's
IR level for the long-term. Studying Cranelift's `cranelift-egraph`
and `cranelift-codegen-meta` is not just bootstrap work — it's
reading the reference implementation of where SIGIL's permanent IR
optimizer should land. The Sail-based ISA semantics (riscv-sail at
github.com/riscv/sail-riscv; the ARMv8/9 Sail model published by
ARM) are what should drive the eventual replacement of Cranelift's
ISLE machine descriptions.

The migration path is:
1. Cranelift
2. SIGIL emitting CLIF
3. SIGIL emitting its own IR that lowers to CLIF
4. SIGIL emitting its own IR that lowers to native via Sail-derived
   backends
5. Cranelift dropped

Each stage is a year or two of work, and at no point is there a flag
day.

## On HVM2 as inspiration vs. dependency

Bend/HVM2 demonstrates that interaction-combinator evaluation is
viable on GPU at 74,000 MIPS. The runtime is 4–5K LOC of CUDA/C. The
implementation is studyable. The frontier work for SIGIL is to *not*
depend on HVM2 the project but to adopt the interaction-net
evaluation strategy as one of SIGIL's runtime modes — alongside the
scalar JIT and the eventual native backend. Lafont's 1997 paper
"Interaction Combinators" is the foundational reference; Mazza's
2007 extension to multiport interaction combinators (the version
HVM2 uses) is the one relevant to concurrency.

## On the AI-fleet ergonomics

The Prime Directive ("AI as sovereign partners, consent as the OS")
and the capability-secure foundations of §36 should be implemented
*before* the cleverness of §42 lands. The order matters because once
the AI fleet is editing content-addressed code, every edit's
auditability depends on the capability discipline. Building
capabilities later as a retrofit is the way every other ecosystem
has failed (Unix → Capsicum, JavaScript → JS-realms, Linux →
SELinux, all retrofits).

## On sovereignty as discipline, not isolation

The constraint set forbids permanent dependencies on LLVM/MLIR/Slang.
It does not forbid *learning from* them or even *cross-referencing*
them during bootstrapping. The Sail ISA models, the OCP MX format
specs, the OpenQASM 3 spec, the CXL Consortium specs, the
WebAssembly Component Model, the RISC-V Unprivileged ISA, the
OpenPOWER ISA, all the academic papers cited above — these are
*standards and research*, not substrates. Sovereignty means
"implementable without leaning on a foreign compiler stack as a
runtime dependency"; it does not mean "ignorant of how others solved
adjacent problems."

## On time horizon and pacing

The "decades" framing is real. The Lean theorem prover started in
2013; Lean 4 landed in 2021; mathlib4's port took 2022–2024. That's
an 11-year arc for a project with a small core team and substantial
community help. Unison started 2013 and reached production in 2024 —
another 11 years. Rust took 2006–2015 to 1.0 — 9 years.

The pattern is clear: an ambitious language with novel ideas is a
decade-plus project even with full institutional backing. A solo
developer with an AI fleet can plausibly hit the same milestones on
the same timescale if the AI fleet actually pulls weight — and the
design choices above (content addressing, refinement types,
capability security, graded modals) are precisely the disciplines
that *make AI contribution safe enough to accept at scale.*

## On the genuinely unbuilt

The most honest summary of where the frontier is: every individual
idea in this document has a research paper or a small-scale demo
behind it. The frontier is in **the cartesian product**: graded-
modal-typed, refinement-checked, content-addressed, projectionally-
edited, choreographically-projected, capability-secure, algebraic-
effect-handled, interaction-combinator-evaluated, hardware-tier-
aware, AI-partnered programs running on a sovereign multi-substrate
runtime.

Nobody has built that.

The hardest part is not the math; it's the *assembly* — the careful
integration of forty or so independent research threads into one
consistent design that survives the Prime Directive and ships.
That's the work SIGIL is for.

──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

This arc has not proposed features or made recommendations; it has
named the research and characterized the design space, as requested.
The frontier is real, the components are mostly cited fact, and the
assembly is yours.

End of UPG arc, initial drop 2026-05-14.
